  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 956 PM MDT Sun Oct 18 2026\n\nISOLATED SHOWERS AND SEASONABLE TEMPS TO BEGIN THE WORK WEEK:\n\nBroad southwesterly flow will dominate the start of the work week, \nmaintaining moisture advection across the region. Expect increasing \ncloud cover along with isolated showers through Tuesday. Modest warm \nair advection will nudge high temperatures closer to seasonal \nnormals despite the increased cloud cover. While localized breezy \nconditions could accompany collapsing showers, organized convection \nis not anticipated.\n\nWIDESPREAD RAIN RETURNS LATE-WEEK: \n\nBy Wednesday, a strong low pressure system drops southward along the \nCalifornia coast, while an upper level jet noses into the Four \nCorners region. These two features will work in tandem to bring \nremnant moisture from what is now Hurricane Trudy into the region, \nbringing the return of widespread precipitation chances. Initial \nmodel solutions depict the most likely time period for\nwidespread showers to be Wednesday night through Thursday \nnight. It is also important to note that a large majority of the\nprecipitation from this system will fall as rain, with the pool\nof colder temperatures staying well north of the forecast area.\nAs a result, snow levels will remain high, generally at or \nabove 12,000 feet. Given the prospect of high-elevation rain \nfalling onto antecedent snowpack and saturated soils from last \nweek's precipitation, flash flooding and hydrologic impacts will\nbe monitored closely as the system progresses. Ensemble \nguidance supports PWATs upwards of 250-300% of normal, providing\nanother strong signal to monitor for potential impacts. Keep in\nmind that the forecast remains fluid this far out, and key \ndetails may shift over the coming days. Stay tuned for updates. \n\nPrecipitation chances continue into Friday afternoon, though \ncoverage will gradually taper off. Meanwhile, the low pressure \nsystem will be absorbed into the main flow, driving a frontal \npassage through the area. This will bring cooler temperatures on \nFriday, dropping highs by 5 to 10 degrees compared to Thursday and \npotentially taking overnight temperatures back below freezing.",
